namespace trion.propositional:
    primitive truth example
    primitive 48, alternative JSON key order, canonical document lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest, canonical JSON, canonical query lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest
    primitive declaration order, document identifier, duplicate JSON field, duplicate name, formula depth
    primitive insignificant whitespace, issued artifact, lowercase name, malformed payload, missing JSON field
    primitive name spelling, operand order, ordinary JSON whitespace, premise duplicate, premise order
    primitive unknown JSON field, unsupported version, uppercase, UTF-8

    source PROP-JSON:
        title "classical-propositional/1 canonical Core JSON"
        locator "urn:trion:core-json:1"
        kind citation
        quote DOCUMENT """
document = {"semantics":"classical-propositional/1","schema":"core-json/1","definitions":"empty/1","declarations":[name,...],"premises":[expr,...]}
expr = "true" | "false" | {"atom":u32} | {"not":expr} | {"and":[expr,expr]} | {"or":[expr,expr]}
"""
        quote QUERY """
query = {"document":document_id,"question":question}
question = "satisfiable" | {"entails":expr}
problem = {"document":document,"query":query}
"""
        quote ENCODING """
Object keys appear in the order shown. Lists can be empty.
Both integer slots and declaration offsets start at zero.
Names match [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]* and are case-sensitive.
Names have no normalization or namespace lookup.
Canonical bytes are UTF-8 without insignificant whitespace or a trailing newline.
The digest is SHA-256 in 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits.
"""
        quote TRUTH """
True evaluates to true under the empty valuation.
False evaluates to false under the empty valuation.
Atom(P) evaluates to false when P has value false.
Not(False) evaluates to true.
And(True,False) evaluates to false.
Or(True,False) evaluates to true.
Implies(False,False) evaluates to true.
"""
    source PROP-MODEL:
        title "Propositional metatheory and mathematical model"
        locator "../../formal/Trion/Propositional.lean"
        kind citation

    section "A finite tree and a declaration table":
        cite FOUNDATION
        A declaration table means an ordered list of proposition names.
        A proposition name means an ASCII name that matches "[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]*".
        A slot means the zero-based index of a name in the declaration table.
        A valuation means a total function from slots to Boolean values.
        A formula means a finite tree of propositional constructors.
        The definition environment of propositional analysis is empty.
        The premises of a document denote a set of formulas.
        The constructor of a formula is truth literal, falsity literal, proposition reference, negation operator, conjunction operator, or disjunction operator.

        [PROP-NAMES]
        The propositional core shall distinguish uppercase and lowercase names.
        The propositional core shall preserve the spelling of each name.
        The propositional core shall reject each duplicate name.
        The propositional core shall reject each undeclared slot.

        [PROP-EMPTY]
        An empty declaration table has exactly one valuation.

        [PROP-UNCONSTRAINED]
        A declaration imposes no truth value.

        [PROP-PROFILE]
        If the depth of a formula exceeds 48, then the propositional core shall reject the formula.

    section "Truth at a valuation":
        cite FOUNDATION
        [PROP-TRUE]
        Every valuation satisfies the truth constant.

        [PROP-FALSE]
        No valuation satisfies the falsity constant.

        [PROP-ATOM]
        An atom is true exactly when the value of the slot of the atom is true.

        [PROP-NOT]
        A negation is true exactly when the operand of the negation is false.

        [PROP-AND]
        A conjunction is true exactly when both operands are true.

        [PROP-OR]
        A disjunction is true exactly when at least one operand is true.

        [PROP-IMPLIES]
        An implication is true exactly when the antecedent of the implication is false or the consequent of the implication is true.

        [PROP-TRUTH-EXAMPLES]
        The propositional checker shall check the truth examples.
            cite PROP-JSON#TRUTH

        [PROP-EXPAND]
        The propositional parser shall expand an implication to a disjunction of the negation of the antecedent and the consequent.
            cite PROP-MODEL

    section "Immutable artifacts and canonical JSON":
        cite FOUNDATION
        [PROP-JSON-WRITE]
        The propositional core shall write a document in canonical JSON.
            cite PROP-JSON#DOCUMENT
        The propositional core shall write a query in canonical JSON.
            cite PROP-JSON#QUERY
        The propositional core shall write canonical JSON as UTF-8.
            cite PROP-JSON#ENCODING
        The propositional core shall omit insignificant whitespace from canonical JSON.
        The propositional core shall preserve the declaration order.
        The propositional core shall preserve the premise order.
        The propositional core shall preserve premise duplicates.
        The propositional core shall preserve the operand order.

        [PROP-JSON-READ]
        The propositional core shall reject each unknown JSON field.
        The propositional core shall reject each duplicate JSON field.
        The propositional core shall reject each missing JSON field.
        The propositional core shall reject each unsupported version.
        The propositional core shall reject each malformed payload.

        [PROP-IDENTITY]
        The propositional core shall identify a document using the lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest of the canonical document.
        The propositional core shall identify a query using the lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest of the canonical query.
        The propositional core shall exclude source locations from a document identifier.
        The propositional core shall preserve an issued artifact.
        The propositional core shall accept ordinary JSON whitespace.
        The propositional core shall accept alternative JSON key orders.
